  "title": {
    "en": "Southern overshoots and autumn falls"
  },
  "subtitle": {
    "en": "Hoopoe, Bee-eater, Woodchat Shrike, Squacco Heron, Glossy Ibis, Subalpine Warbler, Rosefinch and Wryneck"
  },
  "intro": {
    "en": "When a warm spell and south winds arrive in April and May, a few birds from the Mediterranean fly past their breeding grounds and arrive on the coasts of north-west Europe. These are 'overshoots'. Hoopoes, Bee-eaters, Woodchat Shrikes and Subalpine Warblers are the best-known, and each year a handful appear in southern England, the Netherlands and Denmark. Some of them stay to breed in a warm year.\n\nIn autumn the pattern is different: east winds and low cloud in September bring a 'fall' of migrants from Scandinavia: thousands of thrushes, warblers and flycatchers on the east coast overnight. Rosefinches and Wrynecks are two birds that turn up in falls.\n\nBoth are a matter of weather. Check the forecast, and go to the coast the morning after."
  },
  "words": [
    {
      "term": {
        "en": "crest"
      },
      "explain": {
        "en": "A tuft of long feathers on the head. A Hoopoe has a large, fan-shaped one."
      }
    },
    {
      "term": {
        "en": "moustache"
      },
      "explain": {
        "en": "A stripe on the side of the throat. A Subalpine Warbler male has a white one."
      }
    }
  ],
